#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128976 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129158 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7a453400-6363-5782-b346-aae37570c9bc" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Complex Eigenvalues of a Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "3958f8c6-0808-510b-81f5-bb82de6a203f" :question/math-academy-id "e-5904" :question/problem "Compute the eigenvalues of the matrix ![](images/11/1198325c2b7aeab6d1c4e5f405f1e979d408c4c8f128851f039202d4fa94c166.svg)" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the matrix $\\, A-\\lambda I \\mathbin{:}$\n\n﻿﻿﻿![](images/97/97e30eb12530173f3d4b7f90aa3dbbf9cef187ef33154f4da600a393c5777b43.svg)\n\nThen, we compute the characteristic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-2(-2) & =0 \\\\ 1-2\\lambda +{\\lambda }^{2}+4 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda +5 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda  & =\\frac{-b\\pm \\sqrt{{b}^{2}-4ac}}{2a} \\\\  & =\\frac{-(-2)\\pm \\sqrt{(-2)^{2}-4(1)(5)}}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pm \\sqrt{-16}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pm 4\\text{i}}{2} \\\\  & =1\\pm 2\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda=1\\pm 2\\textrm{i}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128977 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129159 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2eae4328-dd7c-5f77-8958-840cadb6f7f1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Complex Eigenvalue of a 2x2 Matrix Given Another Eigenvalue"}] :question/id #uuid "2c47a76d-1b5d-5a10-a3c8-aff9647ca068" :question/math-academy-id "e-5905" :question/problem "Given that $\\lambda_1=-2+3\\textrm{i}$ and $\\lambda_2$ are eigenvalues of a real $2 \\times 2$ matrix $A,$ find $2(\\lambda_1+\\lambda_2).$" :question/worked-solution "Since $\\lambda_1=-2+3\\textrm{i}$ is a complex eigenvalue of $A,$ the second eigenvalue of $A$ must be the complex conjugate of $\\lambda_1.$\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\lambda_2=\\overline{\\lambda}_1=-2-3\\textrm{i}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2({\\lambda }_{1}+{\\lambda }_{2}) & =2(-2+3\\text{i}+(-2-3\\text{i})) \\\\  & =2(-4) \\\\  & =-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237941 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129158 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7a453400-6363-5782-b346-aae37570c9bc" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Complex Eigenvalues of a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fae49b2-cd1c-586d-a8c7-6fb4ad86bb63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda =1\\pm \\sqrt{2}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235898} {:answer/id #uuid "1b67df35-c823-5d37-b291-2b556c4e6c32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda =2\\pm \\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235986} {:answer/id #uuid "35fff9f0-620d-5a8c-bdc2-dfce482c7957" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda =1\\pm \\sqrt{3}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236159} {:answer/id #uuid "b5d2abe2-5a67-5dfe-b4fa-665fe56ba2de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda =1\\pm 3\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236966} {:answer/id #uuid "e0af2809-d558-56bb-9ed8-d9ffad16ac35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda =2\\pm \\sqrt{3}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237224}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "35fff9f0-620d-5a8c-bdc2-dfce482c7957" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda =1\\pm \\sqrt{3}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236159} :answer-field/id #uuid "e10b51ec-b3a3-5c9e-b0b9-a50fb5372c57" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237725}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4fbbaa85-f7c0-579d-afb2-474f3846c51e" :question/math-academy-id "q-102774" :question/problem "Compute the eigenvalues of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the matrix $\\,A-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=[\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & -1 \\\\ 3 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the characteristic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & -1 \\\\ 3 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-(-1)(3) & =0 \\\\ 1-2\\lambda +{\\lambda }^{2}+3 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda +4 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda  & =\\frac{-b\\pm \\sqrt{{b}^{2}-4ac}}{2a} \\\\  & =\\frac{-(-2)\\pm \\sqrt{(-2)^{2}-4(1)(4)}}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pm \\sqrt{-12}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pm 2\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}}{2} \\\\  & =1\\pm \\sqrt{3}\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda =1\\pm \\sqrt{3}\\text{i}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351203 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129159 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2eae4328-dd7c-5f77-8958-840cadb6f7f1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Complex Eigenvalue of a 2x2 Matrix Given Another Eigenvalue"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "56def321-6925-5279-8c67-4998b251301d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349242} {:answer/id #uuid "68dbf708-d7e6-5398-83da-492529b2e900" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349412} {:answer/id #uuid "7c42011f-31d8-5f6d-b880-62bc17742a82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349587} {:answer/id #uuid "ef004d6f-d00f-519d-814e-40f126d3c209" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350539} {:answer/id #uuid "fa47d6f9-7ea8-5bdc-90ef-74aca6158404" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350647}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ef004d6f-d00f-519d-814e-40f126d3c209" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350539} :answer-field/id #uuid "39a77da8-db94-545b-923b-0c372e4f5389" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350806}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2a67c811-9dcd-5bc1-8b47-4defccc497b2" :question/math-academy-id "q-81034" :question/problem "Given that ${\\lambda }_{1}=5-6\\text{i}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}$ are eigenvalues of a real $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$, find $\\frac{1}{4}({\\lambda }_{1}-{\\lambda }_{2}).$" :question/worked-solution "Since ${\\lambda }_{1}=5-6\\text{i}$ is a complex eigenvalue of $A,$ the second eigenvalue of $A$ must be the complex conjugate of ${\\lambda }_{1}.$\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{1}=5+6\\text{i}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}({\\lambda }_{1}-{\\lambda }_{2}) & =\\frac{1}{4}(5-6\\text{i}-(5+6\\text{i})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(-12\\text{i}) \\\\  & =-3\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351237 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129158 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7a453400-6363-5782-b346-aae37570c9bc" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Complex Eigenvalues of a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24c289a6-be48-5aeb-b0e9-cefb950a25d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda =2\\pm 4\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348763} {:answer/id #uuid "3ad7ba21-7390-563d-abab-888fcd043928" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda =2\\pm 2\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348979} {:answer/id #uuid "5318037e-c814-587a-a843-01f4cbea5830" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda =-2\\pm 2\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349206} {:answer/id #uuid "dcb823e4-e925-5d52-995c-791b654a2309" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda =4\\pm 4\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350376} {:answer/id #uuid "fabf6aa7-c731-5962-8b4f-791f3fd19841" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda =-4\\pm 2\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350655}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3ad7ba21-7390-563d-abab-888fcd043928" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda =2\\pm 2\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348979} :answer-field/id #uuid "f3ebce99-af06-5ddb-aa67-c9886ee3f42d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351136}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "07c8f901-7555-5b51-8d26-5da48a69d081" :question/math-academy-id "q-81145" :question/problem "Compute the eigenvalues of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 13 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the matrix $\\,A-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=[\\begin{aligned}5-\\lambda  & 13 \\\\ -1 & -1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the characteristic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}5-\\lambda  & 13 \\\\ -1 & -1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (5-\\lambda )(-1-\\lambda )-13(-1) & =0 \\\\ -5-5\\lambda +\\lambda +{\\lambda }^{2}+13 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +8 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda  & =\\frac{-b\\pm \\sqrt{{b}^{2}-4ac}}{2a} \\\\  & =\\frac{-(-4)\\pm \\sqrt{(-4)^{2}-4(1)(8)}}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pm \\sqrt{-16}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pm 4\\text{i}}{2} \\\\  & =2\\pm 2\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda =2\\pm 2\\text{i}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430455 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129159 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2eae4328-dd7c-5f77-8958-840cadb6f7f1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Complex Eigenvalue of a 2x2 Matrix Given Another Eigenvalue"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b833791-e910-521c-87da-41147c8cc0ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430449} {:answer/id #uuid "20b4f533-7424-5726-bf17-08f22b518fc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430450} {:answer/id #uuid "9f63ef42-06cc-58ad-b830-813ec429fb50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430451} {:answer/id #uuid "d29b5d3e-3568-573d-86cb-207f80fa38a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430452} {:answer/id #uuid "dba03e7d-2a34-55c7-a14a-96455d27dcd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430453}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9f63ef42-06cc-58ad-b830-813ec429fb50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430451} :answer-field/id #uuid "5e4f4638-8952-5dfa-8fd2-e5c0fe083b3a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430454}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "408fe19d-d084-5aed-90d7-346f39925c23" :question/math-academy-id "q-81181" :question/problem "Given that ${\\lambda }_{1}=4+3\\text{i}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}$ are eigenvalues of a real $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$, find $\\frac{1}{4}({\\lambda }_{1}+{\\lambda }_{2}).$" :question/worked-solution "Since ${\\lambda }_{1}=4+3\\text{i}$ is a complex eigenvalue of $A,$ the second eigenvalue of $A$ must be the complex conjugate of ${\\lambda }_{1}.$\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{1}=4-3\\text{i}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}({\\lambda }_{1}+{\\lambda }_{2}) & =\\frac{1}{4}((4+3\\text{i})+(4-3\\text{i})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(8) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
